# backend/.env.example — copy to .env.personal for per-developer overrides:
#   cp .env.example .env.personal
#
# Most local config (Postgres creds, SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY, the Slack ears tokens, the puppet +
# tenant seeds, ADMIN_API_TOKEN, APPROVAL_BOSS_USER_ID) is SHARED and lives — encrypted — in the
# committed .env.local.enc. You do NOT need to set those here; you only need the private key to
# decrypt them. Get backend/.env.keys (DOTENV_PRIVATE_KEY_LOCAL_ENC) from 1Password and drop it
# in backend/. Then: `docker compose up -d postgres && pnpm install && pnpm db:recreate`.
#
# Anything you put in .env.personal OVERRIDES the shared .env.local.enc (dotenvx `-o`). Use it for
# personal secrets and machine-specific overrides only.

# LLM / engine / GitHub CREDENTIALS are NOT env vars anymore — there is no runtime env fallback.
# The five dev secrets (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, CLAUDE_OAUTH_TOKEN, CODEX_OAUTH_TOKEN,
# GITHUB_PAT) live encrypted in the committed `.env.seed.enc` and are SEEDED into the dev orgs'
# `org_credentials` rows by `pnpm db:seed` (which layers `.env.seed.enc` via dotenvx). Get the
# `DOTENV_PRIVATE_KEY_SEED_ENC` for it from 1Password (in backend/.env.keys), then `pnpm db:recreate`.
# A deployed org sets the same credentials through onboarding. Do NOT put them in .env.personal.

# Platform GitHub App identity (OPTIONAL) — the ONE GitHub credential that IS a platform env var.
# Unlike the per-org PATs above, these identify the shared Atlas GitHub App, so App-mode orgs mint an
# installation token (with its OWN rate-limit pool) instead of drawing on a personal PAT budget. Leave
# ALL THREE unset to keep App auth OFF (every org stays on PAT). In deployment they live encrypted in
# .env.production.enc. Register the App with Setup URL <backend-host>/web/github-app/callback.
#   GITHUB_APP_ID          — numeric App id (fallback JWT issuer)
#   GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID   — App client id, "Iv23…" (preferred JWT issuer per current GitHub guidance)
#   GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY — RS256 private-key PEM (raw PEM or base64-of-PEM)
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional: override the shared Slack dev workspace with your own (else inherit .env.local.enc).
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
# SLACK_APP_TOKEN=
# SLACK_PUPPET_SEED={"teamId":"T0...","tokens":{"alex":"xoxb-...","sam":"xoxb-..."}}
# SLACK_TENANT_SEED={"teamId":"T0...","botToken":"xoxb-...","installedBy":"U0..."}

# Optional: Langfuse tracing (leave unset to disable).
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://us.cloud.langfuse.com
# LANGFUSE_TRACING_ENVIRONMENT=development

# --- Sandboxes (REQUIRED to let jobs read/build code) ---
# Sandboxes are Docker containers built from backend/sandbox/Dockerfile by SandboxImageBuilder
# automatically on backend boot — no manual build step needed. One-time setup:
#   docker compose up -d            # postgres + redis (redis on host :6380, service DNS on the compose net)
# SANDBOX_IMAGE (optional) overrides the image tag; it defaults to atlas-sandbox:latest.
# SANDBOX_IMAGE=atlas-sandbox:latest
# The host harness ↔ sandbox bus. The host reaches the compose Redis at :6380; the default redis://127.0.0.1:6379
# is the wrong instance (the sandbox connects to the SAME Redis by service DNS, so both must be the compose one).
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6380
# Per-job SANDBOXES reach Redis (their ONLY engine transport) NOT via the host mapping but by container-name
# DNS on the `atlas-bus` compose network. These two are shipped in .env.local.enc so a fresh clone just works;
# only override for a non-default setup. The documented host.docker.internal fallback does NOT resolve inside
# sandboxes on a Linux host (no host-gateway alias), so the bus network is the reliable cross-platform path.
# SANDBOX_REDIS_URL=redis://atlas-redis:6379
# SANDBOX_BUS_NETWORK=atlas-bus
